# infra/.env.prod-local — the local production stack's environment.
#
# **B-212.** `make up`, `make down`, `make smoke` and `make dr` all pass
# `--env-file infra/.env.prod-local` to docker compose. **docker compose fails
# hard when that path does not exist**, whatever the file would have contained
# — so a clone without it cannot run any of the four, and until 2026-08-07 the
# repository neither shipped this template nor named the file anywhere a lane
# would look. Lane A found it by running a documented command.
#
# ⛔ **The real file is gitignored and must stay so** (§13). This is the
# template; copy it, fill the two required values, and never commit the result.
#
#     cp infra/.env.prod-local.example infra/.env.prod-local
#     cd frontend && npm run keys:generate >> ../infra/.env.prod-local
#
# ⚡ **Only two values are actually required**, and `docker-compose.prod.yml`
# says so itself: every other variable carries a default, and the two that do
# not are declared `${SIGNORA_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY:?...}` and
# `${SIGNORA_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY:?...}` with the generator named in the error text.

# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **Generated, never typed.** `npm run keys:generate` in `frontend/` prints
# both in exactly this form, single-line escaped, ready to append.
#
# ⚠️ The generator prints `SIGNORA_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY` twice — once beside the
# private key for the Next.js tier, once alone for the API tier, because
# **only Next.js may ever hold the private half** (ADR-0026,
# `18-authentication.md`). Appending its whole output here is correct: the
# two public values are identical, and this one file feeds both services.
#
# ⛔ **A mismatched pair is the worst failure mode in this stack**: it mints
# tokens that 401 with no clue why. Regenerate both together or neither.
SIGNORA_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY=
SIGNORA_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Optional, with defaults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Left blank deliberately. `docker-compose.prod.yml` defaults each of these,
# and a half-configured OAuth provider renders a button that fails at the
# callback — worse than not offering it (ADR-0033).

# Defaults to `local-prod-stack-secret`. Override only if you need a stable
# session secret across rebuilds of this stack.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Both halves or neither. A provider appears on the sign-in screen only when
# its id **and** its secret are present.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Email, and this one is NOT optional in practice ───────────────────────────
#
# 🔴 **[B-1026](../docs/BUGS.md), second half.** `docker-compose.prod.yml`
# defaults this to `console`, **deliberately**, because that file describes a
# production-shaped stack and production does not serve a developer's mailbox.
# ⛔ **But nothing then set it**, so every journey that registers a real person
# failed against `/dev/mailbox` returning 404 — with a message that names the
# cause and a fix nobody had performed.
#
# ⚡ **There is no local scenario where `console` is the right answer**: it
# prints the verification link to a container's stdout and keeps nothing, and
# no test and no human reads it there. **This stack exists to run the gates and
# to be reviewed, and both need the link back.**
#
# ⛔ Set it to `console` only if you are deliberately reproducing that 404.
SIGNORA_AUTH_EMAIL_TRANSPORT=mailbox
